data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e0ea718b-0b9c-41de-80d1-0b72810a864b","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1790493287,"model":"capitoline-chair-ladder","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"des have sufficient free storage capacity, background services (Curator/Stargate) automatically re-replicate under-replicated data between the two remaining nodes to restore two copies of the data blocks.\n\n**Key Limitation:** While data blocks can be re-replicated across the two remaining nodes, the cluster **cannot** fully self-heal back to a protected, fault-tolerant state. The metadata tier strictly requires three distinct nodes to maintain quorum resilience, meaning losing a second node would break quorum and halt operations. The cluster therefore remains degraded (FT0) until the failed node is recovered or replaced.\n"},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e0ea718b-0b9c-41de-80d1-0b72810a864b","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1790493288,"model":"capitoline-chair-ladder","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":""},"finish_reason":null}],"capitoline":{"stage":"synthesis","done":1,"total":1}}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-e0ea718b-0b9c-41de-80d1-0b72810a864b","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1790493288,"model":"capitoline-chair-ladder","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{},"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":93911,"completion_tokens":24020,"total_tokens":117931},"capitoline":{"provider":"capitoline","ignored":[],"council":{"deliberationId":"c05b95fd-d40f-458c-8f4f-40e246c16d32","strategyVersion":2,"shape":"ranked","members":[{"family":"pro-high","model":"antigravity-gemini-pro-high","label":"Response B","answer":"When a node fails in a three-node Nutanix cluster running Replication Factor 2 (RF2), no data is lost, but redundancy is immediately compromised. For the specific data blocks that resided on the failed node, the cluster now relies on the single remaining copy on the surviving nodes, leaving that data temporarily vulnerable.\n\nBecause two out of three nodes remain, the cluster’s metadata service (Zookeeper/Zeus) maintains quorum, allowing the cluster to stay online. High Availability (HA) will restart the failed node's VMs on the remaining infrastructure.\n\nTo address the lost redundancy, the cluster automatically triggers a self-healing process. It identifies the under-replicated data and begins duplicating it between the two surviving nodes to rebuild RF2. However, this self-healing can only complete if the surviving nodes have sufficient free storage capacity. Furthermore, even if RF2 is restored, the cluster cannot tolerate another node failure, as losing a second node would break metadata quorum and halt cluster operations.\n"},{"family":"flash-high","model":"antigravity-gemini-flash-high","label":"Response A","answer":"In a three-node Nutanix cluster running RF2 (Redundancy Factor 2), a single node failure has the following impact:\n\n* **Data Redundancy State:** Redundancy is immediately degraded. Any data extent whose secondary replica resided on the failed node is reduced to a single copy. The cluster transitions to Fault Tolerance 0 (FT0), leaving it vulnerable to data loss if another failure occurs.\n* **Cluster Operations:** The cluster continues running and serving I/O uninterrupted because 2 of the 3 nodes remain online, preserving majority quorum for cluster services (ZooKeeper and Cassandra). Workloads on the failed node are restarted on surviving nodes via VM High Availability.\n* **Self-Healing Ability:** If the two surviving nodes have sufficient free storage capacity, the cluster (via Curator) will re-replicate under-replicated data blocks between them to restore two copies of the data. \n\n**Limitation:** The cluster **cannot** fully self-heal back to a protected state. Metadata requires three distinct nodes to maintain RF3 and quorum resilience. Consequently, the cluster remains in a degraded (FT0) state until a third node is restored or replaced.\n"},{"family":"flash-low","model":"antigravity-gemini-flash-low","label":"Response C","answer":"When one node fails in a 3-node Nutanix cluster configured with Redundancy Factor 2 (RF2):\n\n1. **Impact on Data Redundancy:**  \n   RF2 requires two copies of every data extent placed across distinct nodes. When a node goes down, any extent that had a replica on the failed node is degraded to a single surviving copy. Cluster-wide data redundancy is compromised, and the storage pool enters a degraded/non-resilient state.\n\n2. **What the Cluster Can Do:**  \n   The cluster remains accessible, and VMs continue running (failed VMs restart on remaining nodes via AHV/ESXi HA). However, **the cluster cannot restore RF2 redundancy**. \n\n   To rebuild missing replicas and restore full fault tolerance under RF2, Nutanix's Stargate/Curator architecture strictly requires at least three independent online nodes. Even if the surviving two nodes have ample storage capacity, the cluster cannot place two copies across two nodes while maintaining the node-level fault tolerance constraint. Full re-protection and automated background rebuilding remain blocked until the failed node is recovered or a replacement node is added.\n"}],"lost":[],"rankings":[{"by":"antigravity-gemini-pro-high","ranking":[{"label":"Response C","rank":1,"reason":"Correctly states that data rebuilding is blocked because Nutanix requires a minimum of 4 nodes to self-heal a node failure, accurately answering the prompt."},{"label":"Response A","rank":2,"reason":"Incorrectly claims the cluster will self-heal the data to two copies, but correctly explains the FT0 state and metadata quorum constraints with great technical depth."},{"label":"Response B","rank":3,"reason":"Incorrectly claims the cluster will rebuild the RF2 data and lacks the deeper architectural explanations provided in Response A."}]},{"by":"antigravity-gemini-flash-high","ranking":[{"label":"Response A","rank":1,"reason":"It accurately explains that data blocks can be re-replicated between the two surviving nodes if capacity permits, while correctly highlighting that metadata RF3 and quorum requirements prevent the cluster from returning to a resilient state (FT0). The technical explanation is precise, structured, and distinguishes data replication from metadata constraints."},{"label":"Response B","rank":2,"reason":"It correctly explains that data duplication between the two surviving nodes can rebuild data copies and that losing a second node would break quorum. It is accurate and well-written, though slightly less architecturally specific than Response A regarding metadata RF3 and component roles."},{"label":"Response C","rank":3,"reason":"It incorrectly claims that automated background data rebuilding is completely blocked and that two copies cannot exist across the two surviving nodes. It conflates the inability to restore cluster-wide fault tolerance (FT1) with an inability to re-replicate missing data extents."}]},{"by":"antigravity-gemini-flash-low","ranking":[{"label":"Response A","rank":1,"reason":"It accurately explains that under-replicated data extents can be re-replicated between the two surviving nodes if capacity permits, while correctly clarifying that the cluster cannot return to a fault-tolerant state without a third node."},{"label":"Response B","rank":2,"reason":"It correctly identifies that data re-replication to two copies occurs across the two surviving nodes and notes the quorum limitation, but it is slightly less precise regarding metadata fault tolerance and the specific cluster services involved."},{"label":"Response C","rank":3,"reason":"It incorrectly asserts that the cluster cannot re-replicate missing copies to restore two copies of the data on the two surviving nodes, conflating cluster-level fault tolerance (which does require 3 nodes) with extent re-replication."}]}],"aggregate":[{"label":"Response A","averageRank":1.333,"votes":3},{"label":"Response B","averageRank":2.333,"votes":3},{"label":"Response C","averageRank":2.333,"votes":3}],"judge":{"model":"antigravity-gemini-flash-low","blind":true},"calls":7}}}

